WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.190
- LegalEagle is coming
in loud and clear thanks

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.840
to Ting Mobile.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.350
Over 70% of Americans say
that they want witnesses

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.570
and documents in the
Senate impeachment trial

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.060
of Donald Trump that
didn't actually come out

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.570
in the House proceedings.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:16.310
And 70% of Americans
don't agree on anything.

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:17.870
As of the recording of this video,

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:19.900
John Bolton lobbed his own bombshell

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.250
that his book allegedly
states that Donald Trump

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:24.400
personally ordered the quid pro quo

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.390
of leveraging hundreds
of millions of dollars

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:29.030
of Ukrainian aid for investigations

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:32.650
or announcements of
investigations into the Bidens.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.420
Some want John Bolton
and others to testify.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:38.350
Some want to prevent John Bolton
and others from testifying.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.254
It's time to think like a lawyer.

00:00:40.254 --> 00:00:43.004
(majestic music)

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:46.170
Hey, LegalEagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer,

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.190
because in this debate a
lot of people are referring

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.240
to historical precedent as a reason

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.320
to call witnesses or not call witnesses

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.460
at the Senate impeachment trial.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.350
But the thing about making appeals

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.660
to prior precedent in this context is

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.300
that there's not very much of it.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.540
When people say that these
impeachment proceedings

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.430
are breaking with precedent
or are unprecedented,

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.530
what they mean is that it's not like

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.010
the only two other
instances that this happened

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:15.890
in the modern era, because
there's only two instances

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.870
that are related to impeachment.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:19.640
But even the impeachment proceedings

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.550
regarding Richard Nixon didn't result

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:25.380
in a formal impeachment in
the House or a Senate trial.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.200
And sometimes it's helpful to refer back

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.603
to the impeachment of
Andrew Johnson in the 1830s,

00:01:29.603 --> 00:01:32.420
that but is such an odd situation

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.040
that it's almost sui
generis and references to it

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.580
can be problematic.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:38.760
I did a prior video on this.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.630
You can check out my
prior impeachment videos

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:43.280
to learn about the
impeachment of Andrew Johnson.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.090
So given that impeachment
is such a rare event

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.960
in American politics, precedent
can be of limited value.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.910
It's absolutely instructional,

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:54.190
but it's by no means dispositive.

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:56.690
In this context the argument that I hear

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:59.000
from certain corners of the internet is

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.590
that it's the House of Representatives

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:03.100
that does the investigation,
and the Senate

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.630
simply looks at the evidence
that the House gathered.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:07.890
Now, that's not wrong strictly speaking,

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:11.480
but it's also definitely
not entirely true either.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.570
And I also hear people on
both sides of the aisle

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:15.310
make the analogy to criminal law.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.890
The House of Representatives
files an indictment,

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:21.060
i.e. the charging instrument
against a criminal defendant,

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.230
and then the Senate tries the case.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.690
And we'll talk about whether
that fits in a second,

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:27.390
but first let's look at what happened

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:29.980
in prior impeachments to understand

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.520
why this one is different to the extent

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.610
that it is actually different.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.830
Now, I mentioned this in a prior video,

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:39.450
but one of the biggest
differences that stands out to me

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.900
as someone that actually
studied the Watergate

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.450
and Clinton impeachments
extensively in college

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:46.840
as a former political
scientist and someone

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.640
who has studied constitutional law

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.670
and separation of powers in law school,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.430
one of the things that really
sticks out to me is that

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:57.560
in both cases there was
an extensive investigation

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.610
by the Executive Branch into
the acts of Nixon and Clinton.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:03.420
And I think it's helpful to go over some

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.720
of the timelines for the
Nixon and Clinton impeachments

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.090
to understand just how wide ranging

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.820
these investigations were.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:12.720
So let's start with a
really quick timeline of

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.080
what happened to Richard Nixon.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.860
As you may know, the
Watergate break-in was

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.530
in June of 1972.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:19.620
- [Reporter] Five people
have been arrested

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.280
and charged with breaking
into the headquarters

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.430
of the Democratic National Committee

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:23.990
in the middle of the night.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:26.530
- Then it was almost a
year later that the Senate,

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:30.090
not the House, began
hearings in May of 1973.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.227
- We will inquire into every fact

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.330
and follow every lead,
unrestrained by any fear

00:03:39.222 --> 00:03:42.400
of where that lead might
ultimately take us.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:43.480
- That was a year later.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.310
Oh, and by the way,
speaking of those Senate

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:48.880
Watergate hearings, during
those hearings several

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.300
of Nixon's cabinet
members, aids, and advisors

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.480
actually gave testimony,
including his Attorney General,

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.200
his Chief of Staff, his
top domestic advisor,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:58.740
and White House counsel.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.870
They all gave testimony before the Senate.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:04.140
After the Senate hearings
into the Watergate break-in,

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.350
Attorney General Elliott
Richardson appointed

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:09.700
Archibald Cox special
prosecutor in May of 1973.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.400
So effectively Richard
Nixon's own attorney general

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:13.810
appointed a special prosecutor right

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:15.360
after the Senate started hearing.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.780
And two months after
Archibald Cox was appointed

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:21.700
special prosecutor he subpoenaed
the famous Nixon tapes

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:22.770
from the White House.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.140
- We will initiate such legal measures

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.090
to secure the eight tapes
and certain other evidence

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:30.640
as soon as proper papers can be prepared.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:32.930
- Of course, famously,
the White House refused

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:35.810
to comply with the special
prosecutor's subpoena

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:37.730
and didn't turn the tapes over.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:40.140
At that point the special
prosecutor went to court

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:42.630
and the case went to the Court of Appeals,

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:47.040
which didn't actually rule
until October of 1973.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.970
And in a five to two
decision, the Court of Appeals

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.470
ordered the White House
to turn over the tapes.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:54.700
But at that point the White House resisted

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.610
and refused to turn the tapes over

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:57.690
to the special prosecutor.

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:00.500
This is in Fall of 1973.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:02.690
The Nixon White House
did offer a compromise,

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:04.430
which I think is one of
the funnier footnotes

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:05.880
of the whole Watergate affair.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.680
Nixon offered to turn the tapes over,

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.500
which were famously low
quality, to Senator Stennis,

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:13.920
who was famously almost deaf.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.710
And the compromise was
that Senator Stennis

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:18.980
would review these
tapes and then turn over

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:21.430
any inculpatory evidence
to the special prosecutor,

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.150
the idea being that there was
no way he could actually hear

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:27.230
what was being discussed on
the tapes, but I digress.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:30.200
What's important here is that
despite the Court of Appeals

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.260
ordering the White House to comply,

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:35.660
Nixon refused to comply with
the Court of Appeals decision,

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:38.130
and in fact ordered AG Richardson

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.270
to fire Archibald Cox.

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:44.240
- The president at that point
thought he had no choice

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:48.640
but to direct the attorney
general to discharge Mr. Cox.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.260
I felt equally clear that
I could not discharge him,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:53.530
and so I resigned.

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:55.960
- Attorney General Richardson refused

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:59.240
to actually fire Archibald
Cox, but as a result

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:01.500
the president fired AG Richardson,

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.500
which resulted in the famous
Saturday Night Massacre,

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.720
which resulted in not only
AG Richardson resigning

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.270
but also Archibald Cox being fired as well

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:11.300
by Robert Bork.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.650
Shortly thereafter
Archibald Cox was replaced

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:15.610
by Special Prosecutor Leon Jaworski,

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.430
who continued the investigation.

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:19.670
- I was given unqualified assurance

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.750
that there would be
absolutely no constraints

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:25.970
on my freedom to seek
any and all evidence.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:28.860
- Now, a formal impeachment
inquiry did not start

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:33.860
until February, 1974, and
it wasn't until July of 1974

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:36.790
that the Supreme Court
issued the unanimous decision

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:39.560
in United States versus Nixon to turn over

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.440
the Nixon White House tapes.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.733
And finally, it wasn't until August, 1974

00:06:43.733 --> 00:06:45.650
that the tapes were actually released.

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:48.950
These are the famous
smoking gun Nixon tapes.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:51.860
In fact, Nixon resigned in August of 1974

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:54.260
shortly after these tapes were released,

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:56.510
in part because the
Executive wasn't willing

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:59.360
to contradict the Supreme
Court at that point.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.390
- I shall resign the presidency
effective at noon tomorrow.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:06.550
- And he resigned before he
was even formally impeached

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:09.350
by the House or tried by the Senate.

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:12.290
So in terms of takeaways
that we all should be able

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:15.810
to agree upon from the Nixon
Watergate impeachment affair,

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:17.240
I think there are a couple
things that we can glean.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.100
Number one, there was a massive

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:20.740
Executive Branch investigation

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:22.870
before the House was even involved.

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:24.460
And the House and the special prosecutor

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:26.630
didn't even get involved
before there were hearings

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:27.463
at the Senate.

00:07:27.463 --> 00:07:30.800
This investigation by the
special prosecutor's office

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:34.700
of the Executive Branch
took literally years.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:36.940
It was also obstructed by the White House

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:40.340
and required not just a
Court of Appeals decision

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:43.780
but also a decision by the
United States Supreme Court.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.370
And on top of that, the investigation

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:47.730
by the special prosecutor was started

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:49.770
by Attorney General Elliott Richardson,

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.430
who is the equivalent of Bill Barr today.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:54.430
Part of the difference
that we see today is

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:56.100
that Attorney General Bill Barr refused

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:59.500
to start any investigation
into the Ukrainian allegations.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.860
That's the reason it's
alleged that the whistleblower

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:06.250
came forward and had to blow a whistle,

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:08.170
there was no action by
the Executive Branch.

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:10.140
That's why the whistleblower
is a whistleblower.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:13.110
The Executive Branch, including
Attorney General Bill Barr,

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:16.110
counsel for the CIA and
White House counsel,

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:18.718
did nothing about the
Zelensky conversation

00:08:18.718 --> 00:08:21.620
or the other actions that are alleged.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:24.180
Another takeaway is that this litigation

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:28.300
involving the subpoena of the
White House tapes took years.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:32.200
It was incredibly time consuming,
as litigation tends to be.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.460
As you may know, in the federal courts,

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:36.320
what you're talking
about is a trial court,

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:37.340
which is a district court,

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:40.750
and then the trial court's
decision is appealed

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:42.900
to the court of appeals, and only then,

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.650
after the court of appeals
has weighed in on the subject

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:47.750
can something go to the Supreme Court.

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:50.450
And here we've seen examples
where that litigation

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:52.640
has taken an extremely long time.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:55.180
Right now there are ongoing cases

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:58.440
regarding the litigation of
Donald Trump's tax returns

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:00.970
and the litigation of the allegation

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:05.060
that the Trump Hotel is
an improper emolument

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:07.340
and is a benefit to the president.

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:09.730
In fact, with respect to
the emoluments litigation,

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:12.670
one of those cases has already
gone to the court of appeal

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:14.800
and was remanded back down
to the district courts.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.590
So it's sort of back where it started.

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:19.480
So it's a long way from
getting to the Supreme Court.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:22.360
And believe it or not, the
litigation in the Nixon case

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:24.260
where the Supreme Court
didn't actually issue

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:29.260
a formal decision until late
1974, that's actually fast.

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:30.970
It usually takes a lot longer than that

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.370
for a case to be adjudicated
by the Supreme Court.

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:35.730
So that's the impeachment of
Richard Nixon in a nutshell,

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:39.420
but let's talk about the
impeachment of Bill Clinton.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:42.750
That impeachment starts in 1994,

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:45.300
even though it didn't resolve until 1999.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:48.600
In the Clinton proceedings,
Kenneth Starr was appointed

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:52.320
as independent counsel in August of 1994,

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:55.410
with the approval of
Attorney General Janet Reno.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:58.120
In the wake of the Nixon impeachment,

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.770
the special prosecutor
statutes were replaced

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:03.110
with the independent
counsel statutes later on.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:06.120
So Kenneth Starr was appointed in 1994

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:08.640
to investigate what's called
the White Water affair,

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.600
which are certain real estate
related allegations related to

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:15.250
when Clinton was in private practice.

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:16.890
And although Kenneth Starr was appointed

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:19.170
to investigate the White Water affair,

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:21.540
the investigation broadened over time

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:22.730
to include a number of different things,

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:24.830
including the death of Vince Foster,

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:28.170
the firing of certain White
House travel office personnel,

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:31.350
potential abuse of confidential FBI files,

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:33.620
and eventually the Paula Jones lawsuit

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:35.870
and eventually the possible perjury

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:37.560
and obstruction of justice to cover up

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:39.420
President Clinton's sexual relationship

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:40.660
with Monica Lewinsky.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:42.650
And to this day part of the controversy

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:45.150
over the Starr investigation
is that it started

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:47.560
regarding the White
Water issue but ballooned

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:50.000
into including something else entirely

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.910
over the course of five to six years

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:55.910
and took on subsequent
controversies as they came up.

00:10:55.910 --> 00:10:58.563
Roughly at the same time in 1994

00:10:58.563 --> 00:11:01.830
Paul Jones filed suit and
accused President Clinton

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:05.450
of sexual harassment when
he was governor of Arkansas.

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:08.440
Clinton attempted to delay
the trial, the civil trial,

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:11.400
over the Paul Jones lawsuit
until after he left office.

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:15.010
But in May of 1997 the
Supreme Court unanimously

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:16.960
rejected Clinton's claim
that the constitution

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:18.690
immunized him from civil lawsuits.

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.720
And shortly thereafter
pre-trial discovery commenced

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:23.380
in the Paula Jones lawsuit.

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:25.410
As part of the Paul Jones lawsuit,

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:28.760
in January of 1998
Clinton gave a deposition

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:30.960
in the Jones litigation where he denied

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:33.760
having a sexual relationship
with Monica Lewinsky.

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:37.600
- I, I have never had sexual relations

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:40.010
with Monica Lewinsky, I've
never had an affair with her.

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:42.380
- A few months later in August of 1998

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:44.240
President Clinton testified in front

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:47.100
of a grand jury convened by the Office

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:49.350
of Independent Counsel,
i.e. Kenneth Starr,

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:51.400
and actually testified for four hours

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:54.070
about his relationship
with Monica Lewinsky

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:57.386
and whether he had
perjured himself before.

00:11:57.386 --> 00:12:00.240
- I knew that in the face
of their illegal activity

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:02.290
I still had to behave lawfully.

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:06.110
But I wanted to be legal without
being particularly helpful.

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:08.330
- You might have heard
of the famous testimony

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:10.447
of President Clinton where he says,

00:12:10.447 --> 00:12:13.550
"It depends on what your
definition of is is."

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:15.150
Well, this is where it comes from,

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:19.330
from the testimony in front
of the grand jury in 1998.

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:23.730
- It depends upon what the
meaning of the word is is.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:26.590
- A few months later in September of 1998,

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:28.790
Kenneth Starr and the Office
of Independent Counsel

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:32.540
concluded its four year long
investigation of the president

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:35.330
soon after the president's
grand jury testimony

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:38.040
and delivered the famous Starr Report

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:39.670
to the House Judiciary Committee.

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:41.780
The Starr Report itself was extensive.

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:44.680
It was 453 pages long.

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:46.210
Republican House leaders argued

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:48.510
for the report's immediate
release via the internet,

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:50.970
which was new at the time,
while the Democrats tried

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:54.090
to delay to allow the White
House time to form a response.

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:58.210
But after two days of debate,
on September 11, 1998,

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.610
the House voted in a huge majority

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:02.850
to release the report to the public,

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:05.830
after which formal impeachment
of President Clinton

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:08.470
started in October of 1998,

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:11.100
really just a month after
the Starr Report came out.

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:13.730
Just a few months later
in December of 1998

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:17.130
the House voted to
impeach President Clinton.

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:21.890
And in January of 1999
the Senate began its trial

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:23.120
of President Clinton.

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:25.830
In February of 1999
the Senate began taking

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:27.690
closed door depositions of witnesses,

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:30.880
and in that same month
the Senate voted 70 to 30

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:32.940
that excerpting the videotapes

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:35.270
of these closed door
depositions would suffice

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:37.910
as testimony rather than
calling live witnesses

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:39.570
to appear at trial.

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:42.720
And then famously Bill Clinton
was acquitted in the Senate.

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:44.500
- Two-thirds of the Senators present

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:46.570
not having pronounced him guilty,

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:48.830
the Senate adjudges that the respondent,

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:52.040
William Jefferson Clinton,
President of the United States,

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:53.730
is not guilty as charged.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:54.950
- So what are the takeaways

00:13:54.950 --> 00:13:56.900
from the Clinton impeachment proceedings?

00:13:56.900 --> 00:14:00.380
Well, once again we have
a massive investigation

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:02.130
by the Executive Branch, this time

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:04.310
from the Office of Independent Counsel.

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:07.040
That investigation took multiple years,

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:09.500
basically five years from start to finish.

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:12.710
And the House didn't really
do the investigation at all.

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:14.040
In fact, it was just a matter of days

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:16.020
between the release of the Starr Report

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:18.260
and impeachment proceedings and eventually

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:20.670
actual formal impeachment by the House.

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:23.490
On top of that, the president
testified multiple times,

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:25.670
both in depositions in civil court

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:28.010
in the Paula Jones
litigation, a deposition

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:31.340
in the Senate trial, and
in front of the grand jury

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:33.640
pursuant to the
investigation of the Office

00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:34.550
of Independent Counsel.

00:14:34.550 --> 00:14:37.070
And as with the impeachment
of Richard Nixon,

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:40.100
it required a decision by
the U.S. Supreme Court.

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:43.700
Even though Paula Jones
started her litigation in 1994,

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:48.020
it wasn't until 1997 that
the Supreme Court said

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:49.670
that the president was not immune

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:51.380
and that the civil suit could go forward

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:52.940
and the president could be deposed.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:55.900
And if we're specifically
talking about witnesses

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:57.500
in the trial before the Senate,

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:58.970
well, there's not a lot of history here

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:01.020
when it comes to impeachment
trials and presidents.

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:05.230
It's only happened twice,
with Andrew Johnson in 1868

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:07.410
and Bill Clinton in 1999.

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:10.920
In the impeachment case
of Andrew Johnson in 1868,

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:12.930
there were actually 41 witnesses called.

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:14.750
And in the case of Bill
Clinton, there were three,

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:16.830
including Monica Lewinsky herself.

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:19.280
So with that historical
context, let's talk

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:21.220
about the analogy that the House indicts,

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:23.580
because impeachment is like an indictment,

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:25.140
and the idea that the Senate tries it

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:27.640
because they have the power
to try the impeachment.

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:30.330
So in the American criminal law context,

00:15:30.330 --> 00:15:33.330
you can start a criminal
prosecution with some

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:35.880
but not all of the evidence that you want.

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:37.710
Indictment is a very low standard.

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:41.060
And you can get more
evidence at and during trial.

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:42.700
In a civil case you get
what's called discovery,

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:45.410
which is meant to prevent you
from being ambushed at trial,

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:48.810
but even there you can
get new evidence at trial

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:50.820
if it wasn't available earlier on.

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:53.510
And in the criminal law
context, you often hear

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:56.640
from witnesses for the
very first time at trial

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:00.220
because you don't get discovery
in a criminal case often.

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:02.530
And it can also be true that
the evidence is overwhelming

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:05.440
at the indictment stage,
but it could also be true

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:08.560
that you want more evidence
at the time of trial.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:11.270
It can also be true that
you have some evidence

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:13.330
at the start, or at the indictment phase,

00:16:13.330 --> 00:16:16.870
but that you need more
evidence to meet the hurdle

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:18.640
that is required for conviction.

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:21.480
The standard for indictment
is incredibly low,

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:25.310
it's probable cause, probable
cause that some conduct

00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:29.920
that gives rise to the crime
or the offense is elicited.

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:33.120
But the standard for conviction
is beyond a reasonable doubt

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:34.690
which is much, much higher.

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:37.390
And criminal investigations
are always ongoing,

00:16:37.390 --> 00:16:41.000
so you might have to file
an indictment to, say,

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.640
beat a statute of limitations problem,

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:46.880
and then you go forward with
trial as new things come about.

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.390
In fact, in many states
prosecutors can actually

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:53.130
withhold evidence from
defendants until trial,

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:55.760
so every defendant gets new information

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:58.410
and new evidence when the trial starts.

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:00.540
But again, these are just analogies,

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:03.690
these aren't required by the Constitution.

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:06.630
The Constitution requires
neither probable cause

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:08.360
nor guilt beyond a reasonable doubt

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:10.770
at the impeachment or conviction stage.

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:13.320
The House and Senate can use
whatever standards they want,

00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:16.330
which is why impeachment is
often called a political tool.

00:17:16.330 --> 00:17:17.870
Sometimes the analogies break down.

00:17:17.870 --> 00:17:21.130
The House isn't a police
force or an office

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:23.850
of district attorneys,
they have some tools,

00:17:23.850 --> 00:17:26.150
but generally speaking criminal defendants

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:27.880
don't get to claim executive privilege

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:29.920
or block witnesses from testifying

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:33.810
at the criminal investigation
or trial of that defendant.

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:35.760
And generally criminal
defendants don't get

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:38.780
to control the investigation of the police

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:42.090
or decide if there even
is an investigation

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:43.030
in the first place.

00:17:43.030 --> 00:17:46.040
So are these impeachment
proceedings different

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:49.410
from the other two modern
impeachment proceedings?

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:52.520
Well, yes, they couldn't not be,

00:17:52.520 --> 00:17:55.290
but that begs the question
that it's different

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:56.840
in an improper way.

00:17:56.840 --> 00:18:00.410
Here, a lot stems from Attorney
General Barr's inaction.

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:02.600
You could say that the House didn't do

00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:05.070
the same kind of thorough investigation

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:08.690
that the House did in the
Nixon and Clinton affairs

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:10.710
or that they rushed the investigation,

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:13.070
but the House has never had to carry

00:18:13.070 --> 00:18:16.670
the investigatory laboring oar before.

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:18.710
In the last two modern impeachments,

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:22.170
there was always a massive
Executive Branch investigation.

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:23.870
And although we don't know everything

00:18:23.870 --> 00:18:26.670
about John Bolton's
testimony, in part because

00:18:26.670 --> 00:18:28.980
he hasn't given it yet, it's possible

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:31.120
that Attorney General
Barr is conflicted out.

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:34.600
By all accounts he may
have buried concerns

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:37.990
about the Zelensky call,
Rudy Giuliani's actions,

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:39.680
and the whistleblower.

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:41.770
Again, we don't know the details here,

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:44.610
but that's also kind of the point.

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:46.330
One wonders what history would look like

00:18:46.330 --> 00:18:48.820
if Attorney General
Richardson had stonewalled

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:51.120
and never appointed Archibald Cox.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:54.910
Or if Janet Reno had quashed
Ken Starr's appointment

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:58.920
as the independent counsel
and never investigated

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:01.270
the White Water investigation.

00:19:01.270 --> 00:19:03.620
Any of the Trump related legal questions

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:06.800
would not have been resolved
by the U.S. Supreme Court

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:09.840
until almost certainly
after the 2020 election.

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:12.480
This gives talking points to both sides.

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:15.570
Some will claim that
it was the House's duty

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:17.250
to engage in that litigation,

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:19.920
and some will say that it was futile.

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:22.360
There's certainly no
constitutional requirement

00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:24.920
for the House to go forward, but again,

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:27.520
that tactic gives talking
points to both sides.

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:29.480
All things being equal,
I would certainly love

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:32.000
for the Supreme Court to
weigh in on these questions,

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:33.990
in part because I think it's clear

00:19:33.990 --> 00:19:35.750
how the Supreme Court would weigh in

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:39.300
given the precedent of
U.S. versus Nixon in 1973,

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:42.830
Clinton verus Jones, and
U.S. versus Nixon in 1993,

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:44.240
which is a different Nixon.

00:19:44.240 --> 00:19:46.200
But I suppose that what you think the role

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:49.070
of the House, the Senate
and the Supreme Court is

00:19:49.070 --> 00:19:51.610
in this context is a political question

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:53.770
and reasonable minds
can differ about that.

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:56.750
But at base there's nothing
constitutionally infirm

00:19:56.750 --> 00:20:00.150
about the length of time or
the amount of investigation

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:02.610
that the House did, it's not required

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:04.730
by the Constitution to call every witness

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:06.730
that might have relevant testimony.

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:09.970
And here history and
precedent certainly diverge.

00:20:09.970 --> 00:20:12.540
It's not wrong to say that in the past

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:15.730
the House has investigated the evidence

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:18.100
about impeachable conduct.

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:20.700
But it's also not entirely right either.

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:22.270
In the past the House has had the benefit

00:20:22.270 --> 00:20:25.600
of a huge Executive Branch investigation.

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:28.110
Ironically, for Nixon and Clinton,

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:30.640
it would probably be most accurate to say

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:33.490
that it wasn't the House that investigated

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:37.760
so much as the Executive
Branch that investigated itself

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:40.970
with assists from the
United States Supreme Court,

00:20:40.970 --> 00:20:42.970
which the House then relied on.

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:46.710
It is, however, wrong to say
that witnesses are improper

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:48.120
at the Senate trial.

00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:49.810
It happened with the
Senate impeachment trial

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:51.100
of President Clinton and happened

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.290
with the Senate impeachment
trial of President Johnson,

00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:55.730
though for reasons I've
talked about in other videos

00:20:55.730 --> 00:20:58.520
the impeachment of Andrew Johnson
is kind of a weird animal.

00:20:58.520 --> 00:21:01.600
So to argue that these proceedings comport

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:04.980
with past precedent or diverge
from past precedent is more

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:08.610
like the start of the
conversation rather than the end.

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:11.550
We've only had two Senate trials in all

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:14.570
of American history, and
in those particular cases

00:21:14.570 --> 00:21:15.960
there were witnesses.

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:18.450
And, of course, it's also
factually incorrect to say

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:22.070
that the House had no
evidence and no witnesses

00:21:22.070 --> 00:21:24.680
when it voted to impeach President Trump.

00:21:24.680 --> 00:21:26.860
They had lots of evidence,
but whether you think

00:21:26.860 --> 00:21:29.320
that was enough to vote for impeachment

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:31.900
and even the standard that
the House of Representatives

00:21:31.900 --> 00:21:35.800
should use to decide if
there is enough evidence is

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:39.560
probably another political
question that I leave to you.

00:21:39.560 --> 00:21:42.440
But whether the House rushed
the impeachment hearing

00:21:42.440 --> 00:21:45.030
and the impeachment decision
is probably inchoate

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:46.740
to the question of whether there should be

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:49.790
additional evidence and
witnesses at the Senate trial.

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:52.500
Again, going back to the analogy
of a criminal indictment,

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:55.570
so long as the House had enough to suspect

00:21:55.570 --> 00:21:58.423
presidential wrongdoing, then it's enough

00:21:58.423 --> 00:22:00.420
to send it to the Senate.

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:03.510
And if the Senate wants
more evidence and witnesses,

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:05.910
or if it needs more
evidence and witnesses,

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:09.370
it doesn't mean that the
House investigation was infirm

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:10.900
or that the evidence was weak.

00:22:10.900 --> 00:22:13.140
It just might mean that
there's other relevant evidence

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:15.047
out there and that the
Senate wants to see it.

00:22:15.047 --> 00:22:16.730
But the bottom line is that the Senate

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:19.590
will soon vote on additional
witnesses, documents,

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:21.550
and subpoenas in the Senate trial.

00:22:21.550 --> 00:22:23.620
And it's more important than
ever to call your Senator

00:22:23.620 --> 00:22:26.400
and tell them that you are part of the 75%

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:28.630
of Americans who want witnesses,

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:31.360
and maybe even the testimony
of President Trump,

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:33.460
as in the Clinton proceedings.

